        "title": "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "5697da7a-af02-557b-bc61-47bce1c16d78",
        ":question/problem": "Use $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}e^x$ and $h(x)=-4\\sin x$, with $y(\\pi)=0$ and tangent slope $m=2e^{\\pi}$.\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
        ":question/worked-solution": "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{\u2032}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{\u2032}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{\u2032}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
